Confirmed: c1d2039a is an ancestor of the fix commit, and no intervening commit touched this logic (only 335006e7 modifies it, adding the missing regeneration call).

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: c1d2039a96068a75f1c576d4787bc427c1fad5b1

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I looked at the fix commit's context — it adds a call to `spe.generateNASAThermoData()` right after `spe.setName(spe.getThermoData().getName())` inside `Species.make()`, with a comment explaining that the NASA polynomials embed the species name and need regeneration after a name change. I used `git log -S "primary thermo library" -- Species.java` to find when this `setName`-without-regeneration block was originally introduced, which pointed to commit `c1d2039a`.

**(2) Specific change:** Commit `c1d2039a` (Jun 23, 2011) added this block to `Species.make()`:
```java
if ((p_name == null || p_name.length()==0) && p_chemGraph.fromprimarythermolibrary){
    name = spe.getThermoData().getName();
    if (name.matches("s\\d{8}")){
        // ignore ugly PrIMe ID
    } else{
        spe.setName(spe.getThermoData().getName());
    }
}
```
This calls `spe.setName(...)` on an already-constructed `Species` object to rename it based on the primary thermo library, but never regenerates the NASA thermo polynomial that had already been computed with the old (auto-generated) name.

**(3) Supporting evidence:** The `Species` constructor (`Species.java:106-133`) calls `generateNASAThermoData()` unconditionally at construction time, using whatever `name` was passed in (the auto-generated placeholder name, not the primary-thermo-library name). Since `make()` constructs the `Species` first and only afterward (in the code added by `c1d2039a`) calls `spe.setName(...)`, the NASA polynomial generated in the constructor becomes stale — it embeds the old name string — because nothing re-triggers `generateNASAThermoData()` after the rename. The fix commit `335006e7` directly patches this by adding the missing `spe.generateNASAThermoData()` call after the rename, with a comment confirming this exact root cause ("regenerate ... because these contain the species name, which we just changed"). No other commit between `c1d2039a` and `335006e7` touches this code path, so `c1d2039a` is the sole introducer of the bug.

I'm confident in this attribution — the fix's comment directly names the mechanism, and the constructor's premature `generateNASAThermoData()` call plus the added post-hoc `setName()` in `c1d2039a` line up exactly.

